BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Cantor Arts] JANE! An intimate exploration of Gilded Age excess 
  and the enigmatic life and death of Jane Stanford
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTSTAMP:20260808T134537Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-52880326677126-52880513704711@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Curated by Associate Curator of European Art\, Patrick R. Crow
 ley\, JANE! celebrates the aesthetic and affective excesses of Jane Stanfo
 rd\, the Gilded Age founder and matriarch of Stanford University. Bringing
  together precious keepsakes\, souvenirs\, archaeological detritus\, and s
 pooky messages from beyond the grave\, the exhibition reveals the campy un
 derside of a familiar biography and perhaps an even more familiar murder m
 ystery.\n\nFrom her elegant French gowns to a colossal painting of her jew
 els\n\nAges: All ages\nSource: https://museum.stanford.edu/exhibitions/jan
 e\nLast synced: 2026-08-08 06:45 PDT
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center
URL:https://museum.stanford.edu/exhibitions/jane
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[East Asia Library / Stanford University Libraries] Moments in the
  History of East Asian Printing
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260809
DTSTAMP:20260808T134537Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53020733796497-53020733924570@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Moments in the History of East Asian Printing brings together 
 rare books\, facsimiles\, woodblocks\, broadsheets\, illustrated books\, t
 ypewriter materials\, and other objects from the East Asia Library’s col
 lections. The exhibit explores selected episodes in the history of printin
 g across China\, Korea\, and Japan\, from Buddhist woodblock printing and 
 movable type to lithography\, popular publishing\, travel prints\, visual 
 encyclopedias\, and the Chinese typewriter. Rather than offering a complet
 e chronol\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-08-08 06:45 PDT
LOCATION:Lathrop Library
END:VEVENT
